WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.969
Every year, Forbes magazine 
releases

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:05.372
a list of America's 
biggest givers.

00:00:05.739 --> 00:00:09.676
And in 2023, these 25 individuals
or pairs made the list.

00:00:09.776 --> 00:00:12.545
It includes people like Google 
co-founder Sergey Brin...

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:15.248
and Mark Zuckerberg and 
his wife, Priscilla Chan.

00:00:16.783 --> 00:00:18.084
These are the piles of money

00:00:18.084 --> 00:00:20.320
Forbes reported each of them
has given away.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:24.124
Collectively, a whopping 
$196 billion 

00:00:24.124 --> 00:00:25.091
of lifetime giving.

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:28.061
That's bigger than the GDP 
of most countries.

00:00:28.428 --> 00:00:31.197
This giant pile of money 
has done a lot of good...

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:34.701
but it's hiding a more complicated 
and problematic story

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:36.970
of billionaire philanthropy 
in America.

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:38.438
We'll show you how.

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:43.443
The Forbes list ranks
the biggest givers

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:45.311
by how much they've given
over a lifetime.

00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:47.447
Biggest to smallest.

00:00:47.447 --> 00:00:50.517
And they only measure dollars 
given to charitable recipients.

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:53.653
That means it excludes money
parked in a foundation.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:54.954
We'll get back to that.

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.724
And money pledged, 
but not paid out.

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:00.226
You’ll see here that Warren Buffett and

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:02.896
Bill and Melinda French Gates 
are standouts.

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:05.532
This chart we're making

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:07.267
measures the givers 
current net worth.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.736
Adding their philanthropic giving to that

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:11.805
we came up with the percentage 
of their net worth

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:13.173
they've given away.

00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:18.511
So Forbes puts Buffett's current 
net worth at $106 billion...

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:22.048
and he's given away 
$51.5 billion in his life...

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:24.484
which puts him at 33%.

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:25.919
Bill and Melinda French Gates

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:28.455
who are divorced but still do 
philanthropic work together...

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:31.624
have a collective net worth of $109 billion...

00:01:31.958 --> 00:01:34.227
and have given $38.4 billion away.

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:36.830
Most of this group's donations 
go to poverty

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:38.331
and public health around the world

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:39.899
and education in the US.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:41.701
And together, Buffett 
and the Gates's

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:42.969
created the Giving Pledge.

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:46.106
A promise by the world's wealthiest 
individuals and families

00:01:46.106 --> 00:01:48.875
to dedicate a majority of their wealth 
to charitable causes.

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:51.478
That's over 50%.

00:01:51.478 --> 00:01:53.213
They're almost there, 
but not quite.

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:55.448
These 13 givers 
have signed it, too

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:57.417
including Facebook co-founder

00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:59.486
Dustin Moskovitz and his wife, 
Carrie Tuna....

00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:03.756
Jeff Bezos’s ex-wife, Mackenzie Scott 
and Michael Bloomberg.

00:02:04.057 --> 00:02:06.126
The other signatories fall around here.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:09.329
Only two of them have made it 
over the 50% line.

00:02:09.395 --> 00:02:13.366
The banker — T. Denny Sanford —
has given 53% of his net worth away.

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:15.935
And Duty Free Shoppers founder 
Chuck Feeney...

00:02:16.202 --> 00:02:19.272
almost every last penny of his fortune.

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:22.575
George Soros hasn't signed
the Giving Pledge

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:23.910
but he belongs here, too.

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:26.279
He's given 73% 
of his wealth away.

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:28.648
The eBay founder and his wife 
go here

00:02:28.648 --> 00:02:31.584
and everyone else on the Forbes
list falls roughly in this area.

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:32.919
Over the course of their lives

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.421
they've given 12% or less 
of their net worth away.

00:02:36.156 --> 00:02:37.957
In the least flattering zone 
of the chart

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:39.125
the highest net worth

00:02:39.125 --> 00:02:41.094
but the lowest proportion 
of it given away...

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.196
is Jeff Bezos.

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.566
The founder of Amazon is 
the richest person on this chart.

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:49.335
His $117 billion net worth dwarfs

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:52.105
his $2.7 billion of 
philanthropic giving.

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:54.941
It's only 2.3% of his wealth 
over his lifetime.

00:02:56.309 --> 00:02:57.110
For context

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.245
that's about as much as what 
Americans, on average

00:02:59.245 --> 00:03:02.115
consistent only give as a proportion
of their disposable income 

00:03:02.115 --> 00:03:04.651
per year: About 2%.

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:08.254
Bezos’s money has gone 
to various causes

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:10.590
like fighting climate change, 
education...

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.760
he even gave $100 million 
directly to Dolly Parton.

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:16.763
Bezos’s philanthropic reputation
at this point

00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:18.731
is kind of that he doesn't have one.

00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:22.368
It's kind of hazy is what 
a lot of experts have told me.

00:03:22.402 --> 00:03:23.269
That's Whizy Kim

00:03:23.269 --> 00:03:25.705
senior reporter for Vox, 
who covers billionaires.

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:27.740
A lot of people speculate that 
that's one of the reasons

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.276
he’s made this recent announcement

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:32.712
of “I’m eventually going to 
give my net worth away.”

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:36.583
There was a lot of pushback, 
a lot of speculation around

00:03:36.583 --> 00:03:39.986
“Hey Jeff, why aren't you 
more involved in philanthropy?”

00:03:41.154 --> 00:03:43.656
This chart helps us see who
the real standout givers are...

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:46.359
those who give most 
of their wealth away

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:48.895
and those who hold onto 
their enormous fortunes.

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:53.700
Even if a certain individual 
is very generous.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.502
If overall, this class of people

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:59.706
just keeps amassing more 
and more money

00:03:59.706 --> 00:04:01.174
we've got to look at ourselves and ask

00:04:01.174 --> 00:04:04.944
“Okay, what are we doing wrong here
and how do we reverse this?”

00:04:05.612 --> 00:04:08.214
All this money creates what 
the Institute for Policy Studies

00:04:08.214 --> 00:04:10.450
has called top heavy philanthropy:

00:04:10.717 --> 00:04:12.185
Where individual wealthy donors

00:04:12.185 --> 00:04:14.554
make up a large portion 
of charitable giving.

00:04:14.687 --> 00:04:17.924
Since 2011, mega gifts 
of $1 million or more

00:04:17.924 --> 00:04:20.360
from single donors have 
been growing.

00:04:22.629 --> 00:04:25.798
If you are a relatively small institution...

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:29.269
that receives funding from 
a very large philanthropist...

00:04:29.402 --> 00:04:32.572
they take their money away, 
you are in trouble.

00:04:32.572 --> 00:04:34.841
This chart breaks down 
charitable tax deductions

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:37.410
by household income level
since the early 90s.

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:40.947
Nonprofits used to rely on 
the broad support of the population.

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:44.517
In 1993, 77% of charitable deductions

00:04:44.517 --> 00:04:47.620
were taken by households 
making less than $200,000.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.056
Today, that chart has almost flipped:

00:04:50.056 --> 00:04:51.991
Households making more 
than $1 million

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:53.960
were taking the most 
charitable deductions.

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:58.598
So just a handful of people 
are deciding

00:04:58.598 --> 00:05:01.234
what merits philanthropic money

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:03.903
and how these causes should be solved.

00:05:04.504 --> 00:05:05.505
Ultra wealthy donors

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:07.774
tend to give to different causes
then the rest of us.

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:11.210
An analysis of the Chronicle of Philanthropy’s 
top 50 donors

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.546
shows that they give to education 
more than the causes

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:16.182
non-wealthy donors give to: 
Like to health...

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:19.852
a lot to religion, the environment,
and international affairs.

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:23.022
That could mean a billionaire 
giving $500 million

00:05:23.022 --> 00:05:25.525
to his alma mater for a new 
building on campus...

00:05:25.725 --> 00:05:28.328
or another giving $750 million

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:32.131
in one single donation to charter schools
in 20 US cities.

00:05:32.532 --> 00:05:37.337
A relatively small amount of money 
can have a great impact

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:41.841
on a specific school district or 
a single charter school.

00:05:41.874 --> 00:05:43.943
The top 50 donors 
are also more likely

00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:46.212
to give through a private foundation.

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:48.114
Most of the people on the Forbes list

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:51.184
have had their own private foundations 
or they give to them.

00:05:52.118 --> 00:05:53.486
Here's how they work.

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:56.222
Donors make large charitable gifts 
to a foundation

00:05:56.222 --> 00:05:58.524
in the form of cash,
assets, or stocks.

00:05:58.925 --> 00:06:01.627
In return, they get huge 
tax benefits immediately.

00:06:02.228 --> 00:06:04.030
But the foundation only has to 
give money

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.833
to actual charities at a rate 
of 5% a year.

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:09.602
That means money 
can stockpile in here

00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:11.537
donors reap the tax benefits...

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:14.273
while charities may only get 
a trickle over time.

00:06:15.007 --> 00:06:16.809
The Forbes list only counts 
this money

00:06:16.809 --> 00:06:19.112
that foundations have 
already given to charity.

00:06:19.112 --> 00:06:20.980
Not the money parked here.

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:24.083
Meanwhile, this money continues 
to enrich donors.

00:06:24.317 --> 00:06:25.952
There's another way 
billionaires donate

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:27.687
that the Forbes list 
doesn't count at all.

00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:29.555
Donor advised funds

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.124
which are more secretive 
by design.

00:06:31.858 --> 00:06:33.493
These are charitable
savings accounts

00:06:33.493 --> 00:06:35.194
that allow the donor 
to be anonymous

00:06:35.194 --> 00:06:37.797
and offer even bigger 
upfront tax benefits.

00:06:37.864 --> 00:06:38.698
More importantly

00:06:38.698 --> 00:06:40.833
they have no reporting or 
payout requirements...

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:42.068
so it's difficult to tell

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:43.870
whether the money is going 
somewhere good

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:46.773
or just lowering the donors 
tax bill.

00:06:49.108 --> 00:06:51.778
Donor advice funds have been
rising in popularity...

00:06:52.078 --> 00:06:53.579
meaning collectively they are housing

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:56.048
billions of dollars yet to be 
paid out to charities.

00:06:56.616 --> 00:07:01.053
As of 2020, that figure 
was $160 billion.

00:07:02.789 --> 00:07:05.525
And the huge multibillion dollar 
endowments of foundations

00:07:05.525 --> 00:07:07.093
means they have a lot of power.

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:08.961
Like the Gates Foundation.

00:07:08.961 --> 00:07:11.063
In terms of global 
public health...

00:07:11.431 --> 00:07:14.367
the Gates Foundation does 
got a lot of credit...

00:07:14.767 --> 00:07:18.204
for their work in improving 
vaccine access

00:07:18.204 --> 00:07:21.641
for preventable diseases for 
very young children.

00:07:21.774 --> 00:07:24.744
Since they've been doing their work 
in child mortality rates

00:07:24.744 --> 00:07:28.214
like infant mortality under 5 
has gone down a lot.

00:07:28.648 --> 00:07:31.083
Their foundation is also 
the largest private donor

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:32.452
to the World Health Organization.

00:07:32.452 --> 00:07:34.220
Which tells you a lot about just

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:36.756
how much agenda setting power 
they have.

00:07:36.956 --> 00:07:42.195
And what kinds of public health 
problems to prioritize.

00:07:42.495 --> 00:07:45.264
The biggest criticism is that 
it's undemocratic.

00:07:45.465 --> 00:07:48.634
Former Enron executive John Arnold 
and his wife, Laura

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:51.671
have been advocating for reform 
of donor advised funds.

00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:53.439
By lobbying Congress 
to require them

00:07:53.439 --> 00:07:55.741
to distribute funds 
within 15 years...

00:07:56.008 --> 00:07:58.211
or for donors to only get 
a tax deduction

00:07:58.211 --> 00:08:00.346
upon distribution to charities.

00:08:01.047 --> 00:08:02.348
Others advocate to increase

00:08:02.348 --> 00:08:06.552
the payout requirements at foundations
from 5% annually to 10%.

00:08:07.220 --> 00:08:10.056
These types of reforms are 
small fixes to the system.

00:08:10.056 --> 00:08:12.959
But if our country didn't have such
dramatic wealth inequality...

00:08:13.392 --> 00:08:15.428
we wouldn't have to rely on 
the charitable whims

00:08:15.428 --> 00:08:17.964
of these billionaires 
to begin with.

